Kept the construction to one pass. The current preorder value can be used only if it is below the limit for the current subtree. After making a node, the left call uses the node value as its limit, and the right call uses the old limit.

Dry run with `[8, 5, 1, 7, 10, 12]`: the call for `8` builds `5` and `1` on the left. When it sees `7`, that value is too large for node `1` and then node `5`, so it is picked up as the right child of `5`.

The input vector is only read, not changed. The code assumes the preorder values are distinct, as is usual for this problem.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <climits>
using namespace std;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
private:
    TreeNode* build(vector<int>& preorder, int& index, long long upperBound) {
        if (index == static_cast<int>(preorder.size()) ||
            preorder[index] >= upperBound) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(preorder[index++]);
        root->left = build(preorder, index, root->data);
        root->right = build(preorder, index, upperBound);
        return root;
    }

public:
    TreeNode* bstFromPreorder(vector<int>& preorder) {
        int index = 0;
        return build(preorder, index, static_cast<long long>(INT_MAX) + 1);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every value is read once. Space complexity: `O(h)` for recursive calls, with `h` possibly equal to `n` for a chain-shaped tree.

Preorder always visits a node before its children. I keep one shared index and give each recursive call an upper limit. If the next value is larger than that limit, it belongs to another subtree, so the call returns without consuming it.

For a node `x`, its left subtree gets the limit `x`, while its right subtree keeps the previous limit. Each array value is consumed once.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    private int index;

    public TreeNode bstFromPreorder(int[] preorder) {
        index = 0;
        return build(preorder, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
    }

    private TreeNode build(int[] preorder, int upperBound) {
        if (index == preorder.length || preorder[index] > upperBound) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[index++]);
        root.left = build(preorder, root.data);
        root.right = build(preorder, upperBound);
        return root;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(h)`, where `h` is the tree height, including the recursion stack.

A stack stores the path from the current node back toward the root. If the next value is smaller than the stack top, it is the left child of that top node. Otherwise, pop nodes until the correct parent for a right child is found.

Dry run for `[8, 5, 1, 7]`: `8`, `5`, and `1` are pushed as a left path. For `7`, nodes `1` and `5` are popped, so `7` becomes the right child of `5`.

The stack is extra storage, but no recursive calls are needed. The input slice stays unchanged.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Val int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */
func bstFromPreorder(preorder []int) *TreeNode {
    if len(preorder) == 0 {
        return nil
    }

    root := &TreeNode{Val: preorder[0]}
    stack := []*TreeNode{root}

    for _, value := range preorder[1:] {
        node := &TreeNode{Val: value}

        if value < stack[len(stack)-1].Val {
            stack[len(stack)-1].Left = node
        } else {
            var parent *TreeNode
            for len(stack) > 0 && value > stack[len(stack)-1].Val {
                parent = stack[len(stack)-1]
                stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
            }
            parent.Right = node
        }

        stack = append(stack, node)
    }

    return root
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because each node is pushed and popped at most once. Space complexity: `O(h)` for the stack.

This uses the same left and right split idea without checking every value one by one. Because a valid preorder traversal places all left-subtree values before the right-subtree values, binary search finds the first value greater than the root.

The array is not modified. A null or empty array returns `null`. The recursive calls use index ranges, so this avoids making new array slices.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     public int val;
 *     public TreeNode left;
 *     public TreeNode right;
 *     public TreeNode(int val=0, TreeNode left=null, TreeNode right=null) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
public class Solution {
    public TreeNode BstFromPreorder(int[] preorder) {
        if (preorder == null || preorder.Length == 0) {
            return null;
        }

        return Build(preorder, 0, preorder.Length - 1);
    }

    private TreeNode Build(int[] preorder, int start, int end) {
        if (start > end) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[start]);
        int low = start + 1;
        int high = end + 1;

        while (low < high) {
            int middle = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (preorder[middle] > root.val) {
                high = middle;
            } else {
                low = middle + 1;
            }
        }

        root.left = Build(preorder, start + 1, low - 1);
        root.right = Build(preorder, low, end);
        return root;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for the binary searches across the recursive levels. Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursion stack.

